        "original_id": "7da78daf-44e7-5427-883b-70d5d4e8e517",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gararish, Semites, Semitic peoples\n- Places: Wadi Haifa, Merawi, United States\n- Sources: 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica\n- Concepts: Semitic origin, Semitic languages, Semitic cultures, Semitic Neopaganism\n- Religions: old religious traditions of the Semitic peoples\n\nFacts:\n- The 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica described the Gararish as a semi-nomadic, semi-agricultural tribe of Semitic origin.\n- The 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica located the Gararish further north between Wadi Haifa and Merawi.\n- Semites, Semitic people, or Semitic cultures was a term for an ethnic, cultural, or racial group who speak or spoke the Semitic languages.\n- Semitic Neopaganism refers to a group of religions based on or attempting to reconstruct the old religious traditions of the Semitic peoples.\n- Semitic Neopaganism is mostly practiced among secular Jews in the United States.",
